# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
# ...but the template carries no secrets and must be committed.
!.env.local.example

# The requirements spec stays local. It is the working planning document and
# its §14 names internal Visa artifacts (review PDFs, the _How_/_What_
# assessments, internal README filenames) in order to rule them OUT of scope.
# That discussion is useful to keep and unsafe to publish in a public repo,
# which is the same "if in doubt, leave it out" rule the section itself states.
/REQUIREMENTS.html

# Reference copies of the old static site. Kept locally for layout reference
# (REQUIREMENTS.html §8) but deliberately NOT published: the repo is public,
# and these files contain a scrapeable email address plus facts that have since
# been corrected (CGPA, GitHub and LinkedIn handles). Publishing them would
# undo decisions L-6 and C-1/C-2.
/_legacy/

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts
